A||N||16||Coop88||Brake Noise||||5||
Z||000000||Coop88||10-26-2000||08:39 PM||||When stopped, my front brakes on my 88 formula "creak" when I press the brake pedal. I lubed the caliper slide pins with antisieze, and did the same with the "pad pins"(the ones you have to punch out when changing the pads). While having a helper push the brake pedal, I see the rotors being pulled or tugged on by the calipers. Is this normal? There is also some "feedback" in the brake pedal. Anyone have any ideas what could be causing this sound? Car is fine while driving, this just happens when stopped. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula||10-26-2000||09:08 PM||||mine do the same thing and EVERYTHING is NEW, I have no clue what it is||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||10-26-2000||09:28 PM||||Hey Coop!<P>What kind of feedback are you talking about? Pulsating pedal? Means warped rotor! Have you had tires off for any reason? If they have been off, the torque an the lugs may be off or they were tightened unevenly. My '88 is real picky about that. The 'creaking' is probably glazed rotors. Have you been just city driving lately?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Coop88||10-27-2000||12:13 AM||||Ok, how do I say this.... I can "feel" the creaking in my brake pedal. There is no pulsation while I'm braking. My brakes only creak when the car is standing still(or maybe while I'm rolling too, and I can't hear?). I'm gonna check the torque, though. Is it normal for the caliper to pull on the rotor, though?||||reg||10
Z||000004||kinboyatuwo||10-27-2000||10:21 AM||||I have the same thing, if anyone knows, tell us how to stop it.  It e=worried me at first but now i am okay with it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||10-27-2000||11:33 AM||||Not normally, but I think it could if the wheels are tightened unevenly. <BR>Loosen them up, raise the car, finish loosening them and retighten in a 5 star pattern and lower the car and retorque in 5 star pattern. Aluminum wheels are 100 ft. lbs. See if that helps. Might as well look and see if you have heavy glazing while in the air and the tire is loose.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Coop88||10-28-2000||02:10 PM||||Ok. I had the wheels off last night. The brakes will still creak without the wheels, and when they are torqued down. I think it might be coming from the piston pressing against the brake pad, or from the caliper itself flexing. I'll play with it some more tomorrow. The rear doesn't make any noise and the only thing it has that the front doesn't are the new calipers. Hhmmmm....||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-28-2000||02:25 PM||||You can feel it in the pedal.... did you check the pivots on the pedal and linkage? Those can do that if the bushings are shot.||||reg||1
Z||000008||wwh0001||10-30-2000||01:13 PM||||My 88 foumula does the same thing. The creaking is coming from the calipers. Have put in new sliders ect and they still creak. Sure hope someone comes up with the answer on this one. Has nothing to do with rotos, linkage. The sound comes when the brake pedal is pressed with or without rotors. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Coop88||10-30-2000||09:14 PM||||No solution yet. I'm gonna see what I can figure out Thursday. Does anyone think it's possible that the caliper could be flexing? Has everyone else used that anti-squal stuff on their pads? Just curious.||||reg||10
Z||000010||robertjay||10-31-2000||07:58 AM||||I used the anti squeal goop and mine still creeks when I apply my brake.  I would really like to konw what is making the noise too.  Later!||||reg||1
Z||000011||ChadMan||10-31-2000||08:38 AM||||My original 88 calipers squeaked.  The rebuilt Fiero Store calipers don't.  <P>My guess is that your sliders are sticking in the calipers - I didn't know until I ordered the rebuilt calipers that they are supposed to be able to move back and forth in the caliper in addition to sliding on the pins.  The sliders in 3 of my 4 original calipers were jammed tight.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||10-31-2000||10:17 AM||||My Formula did the same thing. I replaced calipers with FieroStore rebuilt calipers, and replaced brake pads. No more creaking. Now, to replace the rears.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Alex4mula||10-31-2000||11:40 AM||||My Formula did the same thing in the front. In my case the rotors were worn out well under spec. Changed to new rotors (from FS) and problem stoped. Still have the same calipers. Just in case measure the thickness of your calipers. If ok then may be the pins problem mentioned earlier.||||reg||1
Z||000014||wwh0001||10-31-2000||01:22 PM||||My sliders are fine, have been changed and they do allow the caliper to move. Rotors are good as well. There is obviuosly something wrong with the calipers , so unless someone comes up with a fix, looks like replacement is the only answer||||reg||1
Z||000015||Coop88||11-03-2000||03:32 PM||||OK. I think I've got it. When I put a screwdriver between the caliper piston and the brake pad, the noise disappeared. I took the pins out that the caliper slides on anyway and cleaned them up. I used some valvoline synthetic grease to lubricate them. The anti-seize I used originally turned to dust.I put some anti-sqeal stuff on the brake pad that contacts the piston. I put it all back together and let it sit for about 3 hours(I had other stuff to do). It sounds like it's fixed. At least for now. ||||reg||10
Z||000016||lawrence||11-04-2000||12:37 PM||||There was a thread about this about 2 years ago.  For my car, there were two things that cause this creaking.  The first is the sliders.  As you may have just found, anti-sieze compounds just don't work.  I've tried most of them, including the high temperature types.  The slider pins must be CLEAN and the inside of the caliper, where the pins reside, must also be CLEAN.  There was some small amount of scoring on one or more of the pins on my calipers, so I dressed them up with a stone.  Also, the boots must be clean, because the inside acts as an o-ring.  Put a boot on the caliper with the slider pin out and you will see that it forms a sliding o-ring type seal on the pins.<P>I use simple silicone grease.  This is the stuff that looks like Vasoline, but is normally used for vacuum systems in production plants.  This grease can take the temperature.  It doesn't cake up like most grease.  In fact, the hotter it gets, the less viscous it becomes.<P>I really hate this brake design.  I've been considering opening up the slider holes in the caliper a bit.  This would allow the caliper to float better, and would probably reduce the potential for this creaking noise.<P>The other place I found for the creak was the pads.  I've been using the CarboMet pads for about 7K miles.  After the silicone fix, I didn't have the noise for a couple years (I only do 3K miles/year).  When I heard the noise again, I cleaned and greased the calipers, but the noise was still there.  I found that the noise was coming from one of the driver's side rear pads.  I rotated the bad pad to the right side and the noise went away.<P>Why?  Don't know.  I tried to reproduce the noise on the bench, but couldn't.  I THINK I noticed that the pad was delaminating, so the noise could have been coming from the little peg that locates the friction material on the backing plate.  For this noise, it only happened when I was stopped, when the car was in drive, and I pushed the pedal hard.  And, the noise went away after 2-3 presses.  I'm guessing that the pad was flexing a bit, and the front part of the peg was rubbing inside the hole in the backing plate.<P>Since I've put the suspect pad on the passenger side, I haven't had any noise.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
